        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: 眼前",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "眼前 — yǎnqián — \"before one's eyes; right now\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "那个事故发生在我眼前 nàge shìgù fāshēng zài wǒ yǎnqián — That accident happened right in front of my eyes."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The new character, in pieces: 眼 (yǎn) — eleven strokes in two pieces: on the left an eye, five strokes, like 日 rì with one bar more; on the right six strokes, a stroke that runs right and turns down, two bars, a vertical that turns up at the foot, a short stroke falling to the left and a long one falling to the right. The next lesson puts it in your hand."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "眼前 (yǎnqián) is built from 眼 (yǎn) 'eye' and 前 'in front', both inherited from Middle Chinese, ngeanX and dzen (Guangyun, in Baxter's transcription), which glosses the first 'the eye' and the second 'before'."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
